[Music]  hi it's michelle from lab muffin beauty  science chemistry phd skin care nerd and  i don't think i smelled that much  today we're talking about armpit stink  you might have seen a lot of natural  deodorants on the market and you might  have been wondering  do you really need to switch is there  any truth to the idea that aluminium is  bad for you  this is the video for you if you like  noting out about the science behind  beauty products click the like the  subscribe and click the notification  bell so you don't miss any videos  [Music]  aluminium compounds are found in lots of  antiperspirant products and the main  reason they're in there is to  stop you from sweating as much and cut  down on body odor these are ingredients  like aluminium chlorohydrate these  interact with water and they form a plug  in your sweat gland  and so that stops the sweat from coming  out this plug comes out naturally later  when your skin cells shed and this is  what keeps your armpits dry with body  odor there are two main ingredients that  combine to make you stink  odor forming chemicals or odor  precursors that are found in some types  of sweat  and the right kind of bacteria mostly  curing bacteria  and also staphylococci basically larger  molecules can't evaporate as easily so  they can't make it to your nose where  you can smell it  in fresh sweat the odor molecules are  part of a larger molecule  they need the bacteria to come along and  chop off the odor molecule to free it  and then it can drift  off into your nose and make you stink so  without the bacteria the sweat won't  smell and without the sweat the bacteria  has nothing to chop up  and cause stank the aluminium based  plugs block the sweat and so there's  less fuel for the bacteria to  turn into odor it also makes your  armpits less damp and so it's a less  friendly environment for bacteria to sit  around and poop out stink  so overall these aluminium salts are a  super effective way of cutting down on  stink  but like with any other unnatural  sounding ingredient there's been a lot  of fear-mongering around aluminium  [Music]  i think this is the scariest claim that  aluminium is linked to breast cancer  but pretty much all reputable sources  says there's no link  so the canadian cancer society says  there is no evidence that the use of  antiperspirants  increases your risk for breast cancer  there is no link between aluminium and  breast cancer risk  the american cancer society says there  are no strong epidemiologic studies in  the medical literature that link breast  cancer risk  and antiperspirant use and very little  scientific evidence to support this  claim  cancer research uk says there is no good  evidence that aluminium in deodorants  could increase the risk of cancer in  animals or humans  the national cancer institute says no  scientific evidence links the use of  these products to the development of  breast cancer  no studies to date have confirmed any  substantial adverse effects of aluminium  that could contribute to increased  breast cancer risks  in late 2019 the european commission's  scientific committee on consumer safety  re-evaluated aluminium antiperspirants  and again they concluded that they were  safe in the amounts used in cosmetics  so that's around six percent in  non-spray antiperspirants and ten  percent in spray antiperspirants  a number of scientific reviews over the  years have found that there's no clear  evidence that using  aluminium antiperspirants increases the  risk of breast cancer  the 2008 review made a very bold  statement  after analysis of the available  literature on the subject no scientific  evidence to support the hypothesis was  identified  and no validated hypothesis appears  likely to open the way to interesting  avenues of research  so if you've ever interacted with  scientists you'll know that it takes a  lot for a scientist to say there is  absolutely nothing  and even more to make a scientist say  that there is no point in further  researching a topic  so where did this myth come from and why  does it keep persisting if you've been  following any sort of ingredient fear  mongering in beauty or  anywhere else you'll know that this is  usually how it goes  an in vitro study suggests a possible  link the conditions in the in vitro  study  didn't really reflect the reality of how  that ingredients used  people ran with it anyway large-scale  population studies didn't find a link  but no one cares anymore because  everyone is already freaking out  so here's the evidence for aluminium  being linked to breast cancer  firstly breast cancer tumors are usually  found near the armpit  breast cancer tumors are usually found  in the upper outer quadrant of the  breast  that's right next to the armpit and so  people started wondering  if some of the so-called unnatural  things we used in our armpits could be  linked  a more likely explanation that most  medical people point out is that there's  simply more tissue  in that area the quadrants aren't all  evenly sized and so  if you have more cells in the upper  outer quadrant that's where you're more  likely to find cancer  more cells means more cancer secondly  aluminium has had effects on cells in in  vitro studies some in vitro studies have  found that aluminium can  affect how estrogen receptors function  since the hormone estrogen has been  found to  increase the growth of breast cancer  this could be interpreted as evidence  that aluminium could be linked to breast  cancer aluminium has also been found to  damage dna  in cells but there are a lot of reasons  why these in vitro results don't  translate to how we use  antiperspirants on our skin for  aluminium to have effects on living  cells  it would have to be absorbed into your  skin at a high enough concentration  but the aluminium salts used in  antiperspirants don't absorb well into  skin  at all one study estimated that only  0.012  of the applied aluminium actually  absorbed into skin  most of our exposure to aluminium is  actually through food and it's estimated  that  the amount that you get from  antiperspirants versus the amount that  you get from food  it's about 2.5 percent so there really  isn't  a lot more aluminium in your body if  you're using an antiperspirant  the study that found the dna damage use  a really high concentration much higher  than you'd ever find in the body  in standard tests aluminium didn't have  any effect on cancer-causing mutations  another piece of evidence that gets  brought up is that aluminium  concentrations are higher in cancer  tissue  sometimes so some studies have found  that there's high aluminium in breast  cancer tissue  but other studies haven't found this  association in a really famous 2007  study there were 17 cancer patients  and they examined their breast cancer  tissue the aluminium was slightly high  in the outer region but they didn't  compare it with other parts of the body  or with people who didn't have breast  cancer  a 2011 study found higher aluminium in  nipple aspirate from women with breast  cancer than those without  a 2013 study looked at breast tissue  from people with breast cancer and they  didn't find any difference in aluminium  between the cancerous parts and the  non-cancerous parts  other scientists have also mentioned  that maybe breast cancer tissue just  collects more aluminium than other  tissue  this happens with some other substances  [Music]  so what do the human studies tell us the  best way to check if something causes  something in humans  is by looking at actual humans and the  more of them you have the better a 2002  study compared about 800 women with  breast cancer and 800 women without  and they found no link between breast  cancer risk and the use of  antiperspirants or deodorants  a 2003 study looked at questionnaires  from about 400 women with breast cancer  they found that women who used  antiperspirant and deodorant and shaved  their armpits  had an early age of diagnosis but some  other experts have pointed out some  problems with this study that would  limit how we can interpret the results  so for example we didn't look at how  often women without breast cancer use  these products  it could also just be saying that women  who use a lot of deodorant tend to be  younger  a 2006 study compared 54 women with  breast cancer and 50 without and they  didn't find that the women with breast  cancer used antiperspirants more  but they did find an association with  family history and the use of oral  contraceptives  so based on all this evidence it doesn't  seem like there's much of a link  between using antiperspirants with  aluminium and getting breast cancer  in humans with real world usage  [Music]  so why is the link between aluminium and  breast cancer so persistent  a number of cancer experts and  institutions have pointed out that it is  one particular research group who keeps  on researching the supposed link  between aluminium and breast cancer and  this is despite other scientists saying  that the link is pretty darn  unconvincing to the point where it's not  really worth investing  more resources into studying it this  group is the darba group you might  remember them as the people who started  the fearmongering about parabens based  on a really flawed study  their publications on aluminium keep on  emphasizing the invitro evidence  even though other studies strongly  indicate that these in vitro studies  don't translate to what actually happens  in humans it's really hard to be  convinced that there is a link between  aluminium and breast cancer there are  glaring issues with experimental design  over a lot of the studies that have  supposedly found a link  and if you study something enough you'll  inevitably find correlations that will  support your case  it's comforting to think that we can be  completely in control when it comes to  whether or not you get a disease  but the reality is a lot of the time  it's completely arbitrary or  out of our control it's impossible to  say what exactly caused a particular  case of cancer and it's usually not very  helpful either  a lot of people who have all of the risk  factors for cancer  never end up developing cancer i'm sure  we all know someone who smoked  continuously and never got lung cancer  but it can be useful to know the risk  factors so that you can improve your  chances against cancer  we know that breast cancer risk is  mostly associated with things that are  outside of our control  some of these are being female being  older being caucasian  having a family history of breast cancer  certain genetic factors  menstruating before age 12 and having  menopause after age 55  never having given birth being older  when you have your first child  and having dense breast tissue there are  also some environmental and lifestyle  factors that  are more within our control that are  also linked with breast cancer  sedentary lifestyle so not exercising  much poor diet drinking alcohol being  overweight  having ionizing radiation in the chest  area and having hormone replacement  therapy  these lifestyle factors are generally a  lot harder to change than just switching  your deodorant  so the narrative that such a simple  change can significantly help us against  something as scary as cancer  is really alluring even though the  scientific evidence just doesn't add up  and unfortunately this sort of  convenient lie can make  us a bit complacent and not act on the  things that we should  actually be acting on there are a few  other myths about why you shouldn't use  aluminium but here's the reality  not sweating is actually fine there's a  myth that because antiperspirants block  sweat then it stops  your body from getting rid of toxins but  your body mostly gets rid of toxins  through your kidneys they put into pee  or your liver which puts it into your  poop sweating plays an incredibly minor  role  the main function of sweat is just to  cool you down not to actually flush out  toxins  and you are sweating on the rest of your  body anyway there's no reason why the  toxins have to come out through your  armpits  sometimes this myth also involves some  talk of lymph nodes so  lymph nodes in your armpits are  supposedly important for clearing toxins  and the aluminium stops them from  working but the lymph nodes aren't  actually connected to your sweat glands  [Music]  so there are some valid reasons for  avoiding aluminium  if your skin is sensitive to aluminium  if they cause a rash then obviously you  shouldn't use it  if armpit sweat isn't a problem for you  then there's no reason for you to use it  if antiperspirants aren't working well  enough for you then maybe you should  look at other options like perhaps botox  injections  if you have incredibly poor kidney  function then you might want to avoid  extra aluminium you'll be doing this in  your diet as well  if you're about to go for a breast  screen then your doctor might tell you  to avoid using aluminium  but the reason for this is that it can  show up on images and make them  confusing to interpret it's not because  it's causing breast cancer so i hope you  like this video if you did give it a  thumbs up and you can subscribe to my  channel  leave me a comment letting me know what  other myths you want me to bust  you can follow me on instagram and check  out my blog for more on the science  behind beauty products  and i'll see you next time to nerd out  more  epidemiologic epidemiologic  epidemio logic

